course: dictionary
lesson: 4556
title: Heaving: force, waves and crowds
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one is right. «Heaving» is a good example. It can mean pulling something heavy, rising and falling, or being packed with people. The words around it are the clues.
explain: «Picture the last lap on sports day: face red, chest heaving, heart thumping in your ears.» Your chest is moving up and down fast because you are out of breath. So heaving here means rising and falling strongly, the way your chest does when you have run hard.
explain: Ask yourself what is heaving and what is around it. Heaving seas are big waves rising and falling. Heaving shoulders are shaking with sobs. A heaving shop is full of people. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Heaving can mean pulling, pushing or lifting something heavy with a lot of effort.
fact: «Heaving» can describe waves or a chest that rises and falls strongly.
fact: In everyday British talk, a place that is «heaving» is very crowded.
fact: Heave is a verb: heave, heaves, heaved. At sea, the past form can also be «hove».
fact: The clues come from the nouns beside the word: seas, shoulders, shop, ropes.
exercise: «Picture the last lap on sports day: face red, chest heaving, heart thumping in your ears.» What does chest heaving show? | Singing a loud song | Standing very still | *Breathing in and out fast and hard after running
exercise: The sailors were heaving on the ropes to raise the sail. What is the sailors' heaving? | Sleeping in their hammocks | Dancing on the deck | *Pulling hard with great effort
exercise: «The shop was heaving with shoppers before Christmas.» What does heaving mean here? | Painted bright blue | Empty and quiet | *Very crowded
test: «Waves were heaving under the grey sky.» What does heaving mean in this sentence? | Frozen solid and still | *Rising and falling strongly | Quiet and smooth like glass
test: In Britain, a playground at lunchtime can be heaving. What does heaving mean here? | Silent and empty | *Very crowded with people | Covered in snow
exam: Kai says «the bus was heaving» means the bus was lifted into the air. Is he right? | No, it means the bus was very crowded | *No, but it means very crowded, as in full of people | Yes, it means lifted up high
